course: dictionary
lesson: 7239
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word pick can mean very different things, so the words around it show you which meaning to use. «Pick a card» means choose one. «Pick the apples» means pull them off the tree. Read the whole sentence before you decide.
explain: Small words stuck to pick change its meaning. «Pick up» can mean lift something off the floor, or collect someone in a car. «Tariq's dad picks him up at nine» means he collects him in the car, not that he lifts him into the air. The context tells you which meaning is right.
explain: «Pick on» means to be unkind to someone, often again and again. «Bullies pick on children who are alone more often.» This has nothing to do with choosing. If a meaning makes the sentence sound silly, try a different meaning.
fact: Pick is a verb: pick, picks, picked, picking.
fact: «Pick» can mean choose, as in «Pick a card».
fact: «Pick» can mean to pull fruit or flowers off a plant.
fact: «Pick up» means lift something, or collect someone in a car.
fact: «Pick on» means be unkind to someone. «Take your pick» means choose whichever one you like.
exercise: «Each politician picked the part that suited them.» What does picked mean here? | *Chose | Lifted up off the ground | Was unkind to | Pulled off a plant
exercise: «Tariq's dad picks him up at nine.» What does pick up mean here? | To lift him high in the air | To choose him | To pull him off a plant | *To collect him in a car
exercise: Ella says «pick on» means pick something off a tree. Is she right? | Yes, it means choose a card | *No, it means to be unkind to someone | Yes, it means pull fruit off a plant
test: «The children picked strawberries from the garden.» What does picked mean here? | Was unkind to | Lifted with a crane | *Pulled off the plant | Chose from a list
test: In «Pick a card», what does pick mean? | Pull off a plant | Collect in a car | Lift up off the table | *Choose
exam: Jake says «picked on» means chosen first for a team. Is he right? | No, it means lost his way | Yes, it means chosen first at football | *No, it means was unkind to someone | Yes, it means lifted up
